from lib89 import *
def B(ch,tn,*a,**k): build('english',ch,tn,*a,**k)
B(1,'How I Taught My Grandmother to Read','How I Taught My Grandmother to Read (Sudha Murty)','Why learn at sixty-two?',
 "Is there an age after which we stop learning? In this true account, a twelve-year-old girl becomes the teacher of her sixty-two-year-old grandmother, and the lesson goes both ways.",
 [("The narrator recalls her childhood village in north Karnataka, where the morning papers and weekly magazines reached late, carried by the bus that also brought the post.","setting"),
  ("In those days the Kannada writer Triveni was very popular. Her novel Kashi Yatre appeared as a serial in the weekly Karmaveera.","Triveni and Kashi Yatre"),
  ("It told of an old lady who longed to go to Kashi, but who finally gave her savings to help an orphan girl's wedding, saying the girl's happiness mattered more.","the novel's message"),
  ("The narrator's grandmother, Krishtakka, whom she called Avva, had never been to school and could not read, so every Wednesday the narrator read the next episode aloud to her.","Avva could not read"),
  ("When the narrator stayed a week at a wedding, the magazine came and Avva could not understand a word of it. She was in tears, feeling dependent and helpless although the family was well-off.","Avva's tears"),
  ("That night Avva said she had lost her mother young, never went to school and wanted to be independent. She would learn the Kannada alphabet from the next day and read a novel by Saraswati Puja.","Avva's determination"),
  ("The narrator first laughed at the idea, but Avva answered that for a good cause nothing stops a determined person and that for learning there is no age bar.","no age bar to learning"),
  ("On Dassara Avva touched her young teacher's feet and gave her a gift, saying she respected a teacher whatever the age or gender. The narrator gave her Kashi Yatre, and Avva read the title at once, passing with flying colours.","the role reversal")],
 ("What does Avva's statement 'I want to be independent' tell us about her?",[("She could not read the magazine and felt helpless.","Cause of the wish."),("She did not want to depend on others even though she was well-off.","Self-respect."),("So the line shows her determination and self-reliance.","Character answer.")]),
 ("Retell the story in five sentences and say what it teaches.",["Avva could not read","She decided to learn at sixty-two","The narrator taught her","Learning has no age bar"],["Thinking money can replace education"]),
 ["The narrator, a child, taught her grandmother to read.","Avva's determination shows there is no age bar to learning.","A teacher deserves respect regardless of age.","Independence comes through education."],
 [mcq("e1-1",1,"Who wrote this account?","Sudha Murty",["Triveni","Temsula Ao"],"The piece is by Sudha Murty."),
  mcq("e1-2",1,"Which novel did the narrator read to her grandmother?","Kashi Yatre",["Karmaveera","Kaveri"],"Kashi Yatre was serialised in a weekly."),
  tf("e1-3",1,"Avva had gone to school as a girl.",False,"She never went to school."),
  mcq("e1-4",2,"Why was Avva in tears when the narrator returned?","She could not read the new episode",["She was ill","She lost her bag"],"She felt dependent and helpless."),
  mcq("e1-5",2,"What deadline did Avva set for herself?","Saraswati Puja day during Dassara",["Her birthday","New Year"],"She wanted to read a novel by then."),
  tf("e1-6",2,"Avva touched the narrator's feet to respect a teacher.",True,"She said she was honouring her teacher."),
  short("e1-7",3,"How old was Avva when she learned to read? (number)",["62","sixty-two","sixty two"],"She was sixty-two."),
  mcq("e1-8",3,"The central message of the story is","there is no age bar for learning",["old people cannot learn","children should not teach"],"Avva proves learning is for all ages.")])
B(2,'The Pot Maker','The Pot Maker (Temsula Ao)','A craft that chooses its maker',
 "In some villages, a craft such as pot making passes from mother to daughter. What happens when the daughter cannot seem to learn it? This story from Nagaland shows how skills are really passed on.",
 [("Sentila's mother, Arenla, is an expert pot maker. The work is tiring: digging clay from the riverbank, carrying it uphill and pounding it, for only a few rupees.","Arenla's work"),
  ("Arenla felt Sentila should learn weaving instead because it is cleaner, can be done indoors and earns more. Yet Sentila kept visiting the potters, fascinated by how a shapeless lump became a pot.","weaving or pottery"),
  ("Village gossip began, and the village council summoned Sentila's father Mesoba. The elders reminded him that skills like pot making belong to the community and must be passed on.","the council's warning"),
  ("Arenla finally taught Sentila to dig clay and soak and pound it, but for almost a year Sentila could not shape even one pot and felt shame and frustration.","a year of failure"),
  ("When Sentila matured, she stayed in a girls' dormitory under a kind widow called Onula, who noticed that Sentila was too tense and showed her how to shape a pot.","Onula's help"),
  ("With new confidence Sentila made a pot. Onula told her to watch how her mother shaped the mouth of the pot.","learning the rim"),
  ("On a sunny day Arenla left Sentila alone with a headache as an excuse. Sentila made pot after pot, almost matching her mother's tally.","the turning point"),
  ("She returned and found her mother dead. After the funeral Onula saw two rows of pots in the shed, one by the mother and one by the daughter, and felt a new pot maker was born.","a new pot maker")],
 ("Why did Arenla at first discourage Sentila from pot making?",[("The work is hard and badly paid.","Hardship."),("Weaving earns more and is easier.","Better future."),("So she wished a better life for her daughter.","Mother's care.")]),
 ("Explain how Sentila finally learned pot making.",["Onula's guidance","Watching her mother carefully","Confidence and practice"],["Thinking skill comes only from one's own parent"]),
 ["Crafts are passed on within communities.","Sentila learned through patience, watching and a helper.","Hard work needs encouragement and confidence.","A new generation continues a tradition."],
 [mcq("e2-1",1,"Who wrote The Pot Maker?","Temsula Ao",["Sudha Murty","Asha Nehemiah"],"Temsula Ao."),
  mcq("e2-2",1,"Who is Onula?","A kind widow supervising the dormitory",["Sentila's sister","A weaver"],"She helped Sentila."),
  tf("e2-3",1,"Pot making is easy work.",False,"It is tiring and slow."),
  mcq("e2-4",2,"Why did the village council summon Mesoba?","Arenla was not teaching her daughter",["He sold bad pots","He left the village"],"They felt skills must be passed on."),
  mcq("e2-5",2,"What did Onula advise Sentila to watch?","How her mother shaped the mouth of the pot",["How to weave","How to light the kiln"],"The mouth was wrong."),
  tf("e2-6",2,"Sentila made pots alone on the day her mother died.",True,"Arenla had left her to work."),
  short("e2-7",3,"In which craft did Sentila finally succeed?",["pot making","pottery"],"Pot making."),
  mcq("e2-8",3,"The ending suggests","a new pot maker was born",["pot making ended","the village closed"],"Two rows of pots showed her skill.")])
B(3,'Winds of Change','Winds of Change: the pankha and Indian handicrafts','The fan that cools a culture',
 "Before electric fans, how did people cool themselves? With the handmade hand fan, the pankha, which also carries centuries of Indian craft and culture.",
 [("The word pankha comes from pankh, which means the feather of a bird. Pankha is used for all fans and pankhi for a small plumed fan of ancient India.","word origin"),
  ("Evidence of the pankhi appears in Buddhist wall paintings at Ajanta.","Ajanta"),
  ("Pankhas became cultural goods carried along trade routes, seen as exotic and stylish. Each village developed its own varieties, with distinct materials and designs.","trade and variety"),
  ("In Rajasthan and Gujarat artisans make appliqué and mirror-work fans with cross-stitch embroidery. In Bengal, delicate fans are made from sola, the milky-white spongy centre of a water plant.","regional fans"),
  ("Palm leaf fans in Bengal are called Tal Patar Pankha and are easy to carry. In Uttar Pradesh the Phadh fans are adorned with gold and silver zari, silk and satin frills, and Bihar is known for bamboo fans.","more fans"),
  ("Many tribes use grass, cane and palm leaves, with geometric patterns and white ink on red backgrounds.","tribal fans"),
  ("With technology, the culture of pankhas risks fading, but it has become a business that gives artisans a livelihood.","risk and livelihood"),
  ("Celebrating the craft through workshops and exhibitions helps artisans regain popularity and live sustainably. The unit also teaches collocations, fixed word combinations such as 'sustainable livelihood'.","preserving the craft")],
 ("Why is the statement 'They were considered exotic and stylish' an opinion, not a fact?",[("A fact can be checked with evidence.","Test for fact."),("Being 'exotic' or 'stylish' depends on a person's taste.","Personal judgement."),("So it is an opinion.","Answer.")]),
 ("How can we protect handicrafts like the pankha?",["Celebrate and buy them","Workshops and exhibitions","Support artisans' livelihood"],["Thinking handicrafts do not need support"]),
 ["Pankha comes from pankh, a feather.","Every region has its own fans.","Handicrafts give livelihood to artisans.","Workshops and exhibitions help preserve crafts."],
 [mcq("e3-1",1,"Pankha comes from the word pankh meaning","feather",["leaf","wind"],"Pankh means a bird's feather."),
  mcq("e3-2",1,"Evidence of the pankhi is found in paintings at","Ajanta",["Hampi","Konark"],"Buddhist wall paintings."),
  tf("e3-3",1,"Sola fans are made in West Bengal.",True,"Sola is the spongy plant stem."),
  mcq("e3-4",2,"Which state is known for bamboo hand fans?","Bihar",["Gujarat","Kerala"],"Colourful, sturdy bamboo fans."),
  mcq("e3-5",2,"'Sustainable livelihood' is an example of a","collocation",["synonym","antonym"],"A fixed word combination."),
  tf("e3-6",2,"Pankha making is slowly gaining some demand again.",True,"Slight rise in popularity is mentioned."),
  short("e3-7",3,"Name one material used in pankhas (palm, bamboo, sola, cane).",["palm","bamboo","sola","cane","grass"],"Several are used."),
  mcq("e3-8",3,"Workshops and exhibitions help to","preserve the craft",["end the craft","hide it"],"They give makers a platform.")])
B(4,'Vitamin-M','Vitamin-M (Asha Nehemiah)','The detective grandson',
 "A grandson is told to guard his grandfather, but the grandfather has other plans. This funny story is also about respect and care for the elderly.",
 [("Ravi's Grandpa, a retired lawyer aged seventy-five, has come to live with Ravi's family in a flat in the city because he is getting old.","setting"),
  ("Last month he took a double dose of medicine and got lost on a walk. He also fell in his garden at home and lay outside all night, so Ravi's mother, Vidya, will not let him go out alone.","why Mother worries"),
  ("Grandpa hates the noise and bustle of the city and misses his small brick house with its mango tree.","Grandpa's feeling"),
  ("Ravi's holidays begin, and his mother tells him never to let Grandpa out alone. Grandpa soon leaves to buy a newspaper, and Ravi secretly follows to protect him.","Ravi follows Grandpa"),
  ("Ravi hides behind bushes, a banyan tree and a car, gets scolded in the park, and is evicted from a ladies' salon, all while Grandpa enjoys peanuts, tea, bananas and ice cream that are forbidden at home.","Ravi's adventures"),
  ("On a bus Ravi pushes to the front, only to find a stranger wearing Grandpa's yellow cap, which Grandpa had given away generously.","the cap"),
  ("Frantic, Ravi returns home to find Grandpa snoring in bed. Ravi feels affection and decides not to reveal that he followed him.","back home"),
  ("In the evening Grandpa gives gifts for his own birthday, because he gives a present to every child in the house. He says his daughter needs Vitamin-M for her memory. Ravi gets a book of detective stories, and wonders whether Grandpa's twinkle was innocent or mischievous.","the twist")],
 ("Was Grandpa really lost as Ravi's mother feared?",[("He walked confidently, bought snacks and gave away his cap.","Evidence."),("He returned home safely before Ravi.","He found his way."),("So he was not lost; he only wanted independence.","Answer.")]),
 ("What does the story show about caring for the elderly?",["Safety and independence both matter","Do not treat them like children","Respect their wishes"],["Thinking old people cannot manage anything alone"]),
 ["Elderly people also need independence and respect.","Care should not become control.","Humour and affection bind a family.","Grandpa's Vitamin-M is a joke about memory."],
 [mcq("e4-1",1,"Who wrote Vitamin-M?","Asha Nehemiah",["Sudha Murty","Temsula Ao"],"Asha Nehemiah."),
  mcq("e4-2",1,"What was Grandpa's profession before retiring?","Lawyer",["Doctor","Teacher"],"He was a lawyer."),
  tf("e4-3",1,"Grandpa liked living in the city flat.",False,"He disliked the noise and bustle."),
  mcq("e4-4",2,"Why did Ravi follow Grandpa?","To make sure he came to no harm",["To play a game","To buy sweets"],"He had promised his mother."),
  mcq("e4-5",2,"Why was a stranger wearing Grandpa's cap?","Grandpa gave it to him in the barber shop",["He stole it","It was his own"],"Grandpa insisted on a hot day."),
  tf("e4-6",2,"Grandpa gave gifts because it was his birthday.",True,"He gives a gift to every child in the house on his birthday."),
  short("e4-7",3,"What did Ravi receive as a gift? (kind of book)",["detective stories","detective","the best detective stories"],"The Best Detective Stories."),
  mcq("e4-8",3,"Vitamin-M is a joke about","Ravi's mother's memory",["a medicine","a food"],"She forgot his birthday.")])
